Questions
---------
    1. 
        a. Insertion 
            Fastest: pushAtBack 100 times -- 5303
            Middle: pushAtFront middle of list 100 times -- 16305
            Slowest: insertAt 100 times  -- 11216381
        b. Removal 
            Fastest: popFromBack 100 times -- 2067
            Middle: popFromFront middle of list 100 times -- 2229
            Slowest: removeAt 100 times -- 5527029
        c. Access
            Fastest: call first() 100 times -- 412
            Middle: call last() 100 times -- 463
            Slowest: call elementAt() 100 times -- 5648135

    2.  The front and back operations were always faster than the middle 
        operations because the front and back were easy to access through the 
        private instance pointers, while in order to acesss the middle, I had 
        to traverse the entire array to find it. 
    
    3.  ArrayLists had faster insertion and removal in both inserting in the  
        middle and back, but LinkedList had faster insertion and removal to the
        front. The back is faster for ArrayLists because only a letter needs to
        be added or removed, while for LinkedLists a letter needs to be added 
        or removed and the pointers need to be adjusted. The Middle is faster 
        for LinkedLists becasue accessing the middle only requires an index 
        while for LinkedLists the entie list needs to be traversed to find the 
        middle index. The front insertion is faster for LinkedLists because 
        they don't need to shift down all the elements like ArrayLists. 
        LinkedLists accessed the first and last elements of the list slightly 
        faster than ArrayLists, but both do the same operation of using a 
        pointer to find these elements. However ArrayLists were much faster at 
        accessing the middle elements because they just used a pointer to 
        access while LinkedLists needed to traverse the list to find the 
        correct Node. 



    Q1: I think popFromFront and removeAt were easier with a LinkedList 
        because you didn't need to shift down the elements after the deletion
        with a LinkedList. 
        
    Q2: I think toString and toReverseString were easier to implement using an
        ArrayList because I didn't need to do so recusrively. I aslo think
        elementAt was a lot easier to access with an ArrayList because you 
        didn't need to traverse an entire list to find the element. And I think 
        the clear function was easier to implement with an ArrayList because 
        you didn't need to actually delete all the elements like in a 
        LinkedList. 
    
    Q3: The client would need to create a struct in there .h file and go 
        through the ArrayList creating nodes for each element and linking the 
        elements together. They would also need to rewrite how they wrote 
        toString, toReverseString, elementAt, inserAt, popFrontFront, removeAt, 
        and some other functions slightly. 




